WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Four prices in one candle. A candlestick summarizes the open, high, low, and close for one selected time period.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Start with the body. The candle body spans the opening and closing prices. Its length shows the difference between those two prices.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Then read the wicks. The upper wick reaches the period high. The lower wick reaches the period low, beyond the body.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Direction and color. A close above the open is often shown in green; a close below is often red. Chart color settings can vary.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Choose the timeframe. A one-minute candle covers one minute. A one-day candle covers one day. Check the scale before drawing conclusions.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Try a worked example. Suppose open is $50, high is $54, low is $49, and close is $52. The body runs from $50 to $52; wicks reach $49 and $54.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:34.000
What a candle cannot tell you. A candle shows a past price range, not what will happen next. One candle alone is a limited picture.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Read it back. Identify the timeframe, then find open and close in the body and high and low at the tips of the wicks.
